load @term/seed/code/structure/set
  find set

# The multiplicity, the single mechanism that unifies proof erasure, the linear interaction-net substrate, and unique
# mutable ownership. Every binding carries a quantity from a three-element semiring, and that one annotation does the
# work of three separate systems. This is quantitative type theory, and it is why this kernel fits a language whose
# runtime is literally linear. The full structure below.

# THE THREE QUANTITIES. Zero means used no times at run time, which is exactly a PROOF (present for checking, erased
# when the program runs). One means used exactly once, which is exactly an interaction-net WIRE and exactly a uniquely
# owned mutable value (the in-place-reuse condition). Many means used any number of times, an ordinary SHARED value.
form multiplicity
  link zero, like set
  link one, like set
  link many, like set

# ADDITION: combine two uses of the same variable. Zero is the identity (zero plus x is x), and any sum that reaches
# past one is many (one plus one is many). This is the operation the usage checker sums a variable's occurrences with.
form multiplicity-addition
  link carrier, like multiplicity

# MULTIPLICATION: compose a use through an enclosing binding. Zero absorbs (a use inside an erased binding is erased,
# zero times x is zero), and one is the identity (one times x is x). Together addition and multiplication make the
# quantities a semiring.
form multiplicity-multiplication
  link carrier, like multiplicity

# THE FIT (linearity check): a use fits a binding when its counted quantity matches what the binding allows. Many
# accepts any count, one demands exactly one use, zero demands none. This single check enforces erasure (a zero binding
# is never used at run time), linearity (a one binding is used exactly once, so aliasing and dropping are rejected), and
# sharing, all at once.
form multiplicity-fit
  link carrier, like multiplicity
